/* generated by tools/build-chain.mjs — edit data/chain/*.json instead */
window.CHAIN = {"se":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","from":"A bank has paid for software it must keep changing for a decade, so why is delivery finished when the code compiles?","to":"What repeatable order of activities turns a one-off piece of work into something a team can plan, staff and repeat next year?","gate":{"q":"Why does software engineering exist as a discipline separate from computer science?","a":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change."}},{"n":2,"name":"Software Processes","from":"If one person can no longer hold the work in their head, what fixed order of activities lets a team plan, staff and repeat it?","to":"Waterfall bets the whole project on requirements that stay still, so what do you do when the customer cannot say what they want until they see it run?","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a project should follow waterfall or be delivered incrementally?","a":"The stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract decide, not fashion. Waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing. Incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point."}},{"n":3,"name":"Agile Software Development","from":"Waterfall only pays off while the specification holds still, so what changes when a competitor ships before the document is signed?","to":"Two-week slices only help if each slice is worth building, so what turns a customer's wish into a numbered statement that a developer can build and a tester can check?","gate":{"q":"Why does agile delivery break down on a fixed-price contract for a safety-critical system?","a":"Agile fixes time and lets scope emerge, while the contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case. Regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination."}},{"n":4,"name":"Requirements Engineering","from":"A two-week slice is wasted if the item inside it is vague, so how does a stated wish become something that can be built and then tested?","to":"Each requirement can be valid alone and still contradict its neighbours, so what representation shows the whole specification at once and surfaces the conflicts before coding?","gate":{"q":"Why are non-functional requirements usually more critical than functional ones?","a":"Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails. Non-functional requirements say how well it must do it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric."}},{"n":5,"name":"System Modeling","from":"Each requirement reads correctly on its own, so what notation shows actors, structure and behaviour together and exposes the contradictions prose hides?","to":"Once the models agree on what the system must do, which structural decisions are so expensive to reverse that they must be settled before any component is written?","gate":{"q":"Why does a complete system specification need several models instead of one diagram?","a":"Each model deliberately drops detail so that one question can be answered clearly. The context model fixes the boundary, use cases give actor goals, sequence diagrams fix message order, class diagrams fix structure, and state machines fix allowed behaviour. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface."}},{"n":6,"name":"Architectural Design","from":"The models say what the system does but not how it is arranged, so which structural choices have to be made before the first component is coded?","to":"Architecture fixes the boxes and the arrows allowed between them, so how do you decide what goes inside each box and keep the seams between them cheap to cut?","gate":{"q":"What decides which architectural pattern a system should be built on?","a":"The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another. Layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down."}},{"n":7,"name":"Design and Implementation","from":"The architecture names the components and the connections allowed between them, so what makes the inside of each component safe to change?","to":"The parts are built to a design you believe in, so how do you produce evidence that each part, and each seam between parts, actually behaves as specified?","gate":{"q":"Why should cohesion be high and coupling low in a design that must survive change?","a":"Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface. The consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system."}},{"n":8,"name":"Software Testing","from":"A design is only a claim about behaviour until it runs, so which check at which level catches a fault before the customer does?","to":"Testing signs off one release, yet platforms, regulations and customer expectations keep moving, so how do you change a running system without destroying it?","gate":{"q":"Why can full branch coverage still leave a system that fails in use?","a":"Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet. A test suite written from existing code cannot cover a requirement that was omitted, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary."}},{"n":9,"name":"Software Evolution","from":"A tested system is handed over, yet the platform, the law and the users keep moving, so how is it changed without decay?","to":"Every one of those changes consumes effort and calendar time, so who decides what it will cost and what happens when the estimate is wrong?","gate":{"q":"Why does perfective maintenance take the largest share of maintenance effort?","a":"Because most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting. Perfective maintenance is therefore about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive, with preventive the smallest. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably."}},{"n":10,"name":"Software Management","from":"Change keeps arriving faster than the team can absorb it, so who decides what each request costs in effort and time, and what protects the plan when the estimate is wrong?","to":"The paper has argued that discipline keeps change cheap, so what must an engineer refuse when a client or an employer demands the deadline instead?","gate":{"q":"Why is COCOMO effort not proportional to the number of lines of code?","a":"Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code. COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one: about 1.05 in organic mode, 1.12 semi-detached and 1.20 embedded, so doubling size more than doubles effort. Duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone."}}],"cdc":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to Compiler","from":"Every program must finally run as instructions for one processor, and hand-writing those instructions for each language and each machine does not scale.","to":"The block diagram claims characters can be grouped into tokens before any grammar is consulted, so what machine reads the characters and how is it built?","gate":{"q":"Why is a compiler built from several phases rather than one translator that maps source straight to machine code?","a":"Because no single translator can be built, tested and retargeted: one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it."}},{"n":2,"name":"Lexical Analysis and Syntax Analysis","from":"Characters become tokens, but nothing yet says which machine recognises a token pattern or why a finite automaton is sufficient for that job alone.","to":"The parser can prove a sentence is legal but knows nothing about what it means, so where do values and code get attached to the tree it builds?","gate":{"q":"Why can a finite automaton tokenise a program but not decide whether its brackets balance?","a":"A finite automaton has only a fixed number of states, so it remembers a bounded amount of context and cannot count unbounded nesting. Tokens are regular: each is matched by a pattern built from concatenation, union and star, which Thompson's construction turns into an NFA and subset construction into a DFA. Brackets and ifs nest to any depth, so matching them needs a stack, which the parser keeps."}},{"n":3,"name":"Syntax-Directed Translation","from":"A parse tree proves the token sequence legal yet says nothing about what any part of it means, so who decides where values are attached?","to":"Translation can now build expressions but cannot say whether adding a string to an integer is legal, so which rules give every expression a type?","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a syntax-directed definition can be evaluated in a single pass over the parse tree?","a":"The direction attributes travel decides the order. A synthesized attribute is computed from the children and rises, so an S-attributed definition needs one post-order traversal, children before parents. An inherited attribute comes from the parent or a left sibling, and when it never depends on a right sibling the definition is L-attributed, so one left-to-right depth-first pass suffices for a predictive parser."}},{"n":4,"name":"Type Checking","from":"The annotated tree carries a computed value at every node, but nothing yet rejects an operation on operands whose types cannot be combined.","to":"Every expression now has a type, but checking a name requires its declaration, so where are name, type and scope kept and how quickly can they be found?","gate":{"q":"Why can a grammar accept a program that a type checker must reject?","a":"A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time."}},{"n":5,"name":"Symbol Table Design","from":"Type checking needs the declared type behind every use of a name, and the tree does not hold declarations, so where is that information stored?","to":"A name now has a type and an entry, but the running program still needs a real address, so when is storage for it decided and by whom?","gate":{"q":"Why does a symbol table use a hash index rather than a linear list, and what does the choice cost?","a":"A linear list answers every lookup by scanning all entries, so a file with n names costs quadratic time overall. A hash table maps the name to a bucket, giving constant average insert and lookup, and O(n) only when everything collides. The price is collision handling by chaining or open addressing, plus a stack of tables so an inner declaration shadows an outer one."}},{"n":6,"name":"Run-time Storage Management","from":"Types and entries describe names but never say where a name lives while the program runs, so what decides its storage and lifetime?","to":"A frame now holds the variables, but the source is still a structured tree, so what flat form must stand between that tree and real instructions?","gate":{"q":"Why does a single fixed storage area fail for a recursive procedure, and what does a stack provide instead?","a":"A recursive call needs its own copy of the parameters and locals while the outer copy stays alive. Stack allocation pushes an activation record per call and pops it on return, so calls nest and unwind in reverse order, each frame holding locals, saved status, a control link to the caller and an access link to the enclosing scope. Static allocation fixes one address per name and forbids recursion."}},{"n":7,"name":"Intermediate Code Generation","from":"An annotated tree with addresses is still grammar-shaped and machine-free, so what representation can an optimiser inspect and a back end translate for any processor?","to":"Three-address code says what to compute but never which register or instruction does it, so who turns abstract temporaries into a machine program?","gate":{"q":"Why lower the annotated tree to three-address code instead of generating machine instructions directly from it?","a":"A tree keeps grammar structure that no optimiser or target needs, and mapping it straight to one machine welds the language to that processor. Three-address code allows at most one operator per instruction with named temporaries, so a repeated subexpression can be computed once, moved or deleted, and the same form feeds every back end. Quadruples, triples and indirect triples are merely encodings of that flat list."}},{"n":8,"name":"Code Generation","from":"A flat list of three-address instructions still names abstract temporaries, while the target offers a small set of registers, so what assigns them?","to":"The code is now correct but repeats work the source never asked for, so which rewrites remove that work without changing a single visible result?","gate":{"q":"How is a program split into basic blocks, and why must no branch enter a block except at its first instruction?","a":"The first instruction is a leader, every jump target is a leader, and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a block runs from a leader to the instruction before the next. A jump may enter only at a leader, so no branch lands in the middle, which keeps the block straight-line and lets its DAG expose repeated and dead computations before registers are assigned."}},{"n":9,"name":"Code Optimization","from":"Generated code is correct yet recomputes values, keeps assignments nobody reads and repeats loop work, so which of those can be removed safely?","to":"Each phase is now understood separately, so how does a real compiler arrange them into stages that share one optimiser across languages and retarget to new processors?","gate":{"q":"What three conditions must an optimisation satisfy, and why is each one necessary?","a":"It must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay identical. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost. It must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied."}},{"n":10,"name":"Case Studies: C and C++ Compilers","from":"The phases have been argued one at a time, but a shipping compiler must group them into stages that let many languages share one optimiser and one back end.","to":"The exam asks which stage a described cost or failure belongs to, so can every symptom be placed in the front end, middle end or back end?","gate":{"q":"Why does an equivalent C++ program compile more slowly than its C version yet run about as fast?","a":"The difference sits in the front end. C++ must instantiate templates before the code exists, resolve overloads, mangle names so overloads get distinct labels, build vtables and emit unwinding tables, while C does none of this. Both front ends lower to a language-independent form, so the middle end optimises and the back end allocates registers with no knowledge of either language, which is why run-time quality is similar."}}],"egov":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to E-Government and E-Governance","from":"The paper opens with a ward office that has bought computers and a website, yet the clerk still decides who waits, and nobody can see why.","to":"The counter is now fast, but nobody can say whether a service only broadcasts or lets a citizen answer and complete a transaction, so direction is the next question.","gate":{"q":"Why is an online certificate counter e-government but not e-governance?","a":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance covers the whole loop: citizen data in, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint feeding back. A portal therefore speeds the queue while the register stays closed, so speed rises and trust does not."}},{"n":2,"name":"Models of E-Governance","from":"We know government should serve the citizen, but which way does information actually move, who initiates it, and how far does the citizen get?","to":"Direction alone runs nothing, so the next question is what network, machines and law must be laid underneath before a single counter can truly transact.","gate":{"q":"Which model of e-governance is a district ranking of schools, and why is it not broadcasting?","a":"Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back, while comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure. Critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records."}},{"n":3,"name":"E-Government Infrastructure Development","from":"Knowing that information must travel both ways does not supply the road, the machines, the shared identifiers or the law, so the next question is what the service actually runs on.","to":"A working platform concentrates every citizen record in linked databases shared between ministries, which raises the next question of what keeps that record trustworthy and attributable.","gate":{"q":"Why can two ministries hold the same citizen and still be unable to exchange her data?","a":"Because infrastructure is layered and interoperability is the socket between layers. Network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices."}},{"n":4,"name":"Security for E-Government","from":"Interoperability concentrates the state's truth in linked databases, so the next question is what stops an authorised clerk or an outsider from silently editing a record that then looks genuine.","to":"Once the record can be trusted, the next question is what ten years of trustworthy records are worth when read together instead of one transaction at a time.","gate":{"q":"Why does a stolen government database differ from a stolen cash box?","a":"The cash box loses value that is visibly missing, while the database can be edited so that everything still balances. Government therefore protects authenticity and attribution, not only secrecy. Controls stack physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit and people, each assuming the layer above failed, and append-only logs turn a silent edit into a caught one."}},{"n":5,"name":"Data Warehousing and Data Mining in Government","from":"Trusted transactional records still answer only about today, so the next question is how ten years of them become one defensible comparison across districts and years.","to":"A warehouse and a mine only describe what was possible, so the last question is how to read a real deployment, name its model and level, and explain why it stalled.","gate":{"q":"Why can a live land-records database not answer how enrolment changed over ten years?","a":"Because the operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large historical queries. Each correction overwrites yesterday, and each ministry applies its own definition. A warehouse extracts through ETL, fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot."}},{"n":6,"name":"Case Studies","from":"We now know models, layers, controls and warehouses in the abstract, but nothing yet tells us how to place a real country's portal on that ladder, or why a well-funded one failed.","to":"It hands the exam a repeatable scoring move, name the model, fix the maturity level, name who lost discretion and what limited it, so any unseen case is answered rather than recited.","gate":{"q":"Why does Bhoomi earn marks as a removed middleman rather than as a Karnataka land portal?","a":"Because a case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Naming fault, intervention, evidence and limitation is what transfers to an unseen case."}}],"ncc":[{"n":1,"name":"Language Preliminaries","from":"The paper opens with a demand to build a long-lived web application on .NET, where every later answer depends on what the compiler guarantees and what only the runtime decides once the program runs.","to":"If one compiled assembly can run on any machine, what must exist on that machine before a request from the network can reach any of this code?","gate":{"q":"Why can the same method call execute different code depending on how the variable was declared?","a":"Because binding, not the object, decides which body runs. A virtual method reached through override is resolved at run time from the object's actual type in its method table, so the derived body executes. A non-virtual method, or a base method hidden with new, is bound to the declared type at compile time, so the base body runs even when the object is derived."}},{"n":2,"name":"Introduction to ASP.NET","from":"A compiled assembly still needs something on the host to load it and listen on a port, and that raises the question of what .NET actually is beyond the language.","to":"If the platform supplies a web server and a pipeline, what exactly arrives at that pipeline from a browser, and what shape must a reply take?","gate":{"q":"Why can two ASP.NET Core applications that need different runtime versions run side by side on one server?","a":"Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine. Each project restores the framework as NuGet packages or names the shared runtime version in its csproj, and the host loads it inside that application's own process. A machine-wide framework would force every site on the server onto one version and one update cycle."}},{"n":3,"name":"HTTP and ASP.NET Core","from":"Something must parse whatever bytes a browser sends and decide which of many possible handlers should answer, and that decision needs a protocol before it needs a framework.","to":"Since every request arrives alone and in a fixed order of checkpoints, what structure lets one message be routed, validated and answered without any shared memory between requests?","gate":{"q":"Why does a request for a stylesheet never reach the controller while a request for a protected page does?","a":"Because both enter the same pipeline but stop at different layers. Static file middleware recognises the extension, writes the file and short-circuits, so routing, authentication and the action never run. A page URL matches no file, falls through to routing and the endpoint, so authentication and authorization must approve it before the action executes."}},{"n":4,"name":"Creating ASP.NET Core MVC Applications","from":"One message now reaches a handler, but nothing yet says how a URL is matched to code, how typed parameters are filled, or which kind of reply a method should return.","to":"A form is bound and validated, so where do the rows it edits actually live, and who is trusted to turn objects back into statements a database will run?","gate":{"q":"Why does a successful POST action return RedirectToAction instead of View?","a":"Because the browser's last request must not be a POST. Returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row. RedirectToAction answers with a 302 and a GET to a safe URL, leaving an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly."}},{"n":5,"name":"Working with Database","from":"The application now holds objects, but the real data sits in another process with its own language, and nothing yet decides whether the developer writes the SQL or a mapper does.","to":"The row is committed, yet the next request arrives with no memory of this one, so how does the site know it is still serving the same shopper?","gate":{"q":"Why do parameters stop SQL injection when escaping quotes in the input does not?","a":"Because parameters keep code and data on separate channels. The statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table."}},{"n":6,"name":"State Management","from":"Persistence solves the last write, not the identity question, because the server treats each request as a stranger and has no way to reconnect it with the previous one.","to":"If state can be held in the browser and pages can be patched without a reload, how much work can move out of the server before that client stops being trustworthy?","gate":{"q":"Why does a shopping cart survive a page reload when HTTP itself keeps no memory of the visitor?","a":"Because the cart is not in the protocol, it is in a store the server or browser owns. Session keeps the cart server-side and sends only an opaque id cookie, which every request carries back so middleware can reattach the right dictionary, while a database row keyed to the user does the same. Clear the cookie or let it expire and the cart is gone."}},{"n":7,"name":"Client-side Development","from":"Some state already lives in the browser, which means the visitor now controls a runtime that renders pages, and the server must decide how much authority to lend it.","to":"A runtime the visitor controls can send anything it likes, so who is making this request, and what stops a forged one from acting with someone else's authority?","gate":{"q":"Why must the server repeat a validation rule the browser has already enforced?","a":"Because the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips."}},{"n":8,"name":"Securing ASP.NET Core","from":"The client is now known to be untrusted, but the application still has no way to establish who is calling or which of many callers may touch a given row.","to":"Guarded code still has to run somewhere with a real socket and real secrets, so which process answers the internet and where does configuration live outside the repository?","gate":{"q":"Why does ASP.NET Core run authentication before authorization in the pipeline?","a":"Because authorization can only judge a principal that already exists. Authentication reads the cookie or token and fills HttpContext.User with claims, then every Authorize attribute, role and policy is evaluated against those claims. Reversed, authorization would meet an anonymous principal, so every protected action would challenge with 401 and no role check could ever pass."}},{"n":9,"name":"Hosting and Deployment","from":"Everything so far assumes a process that is already listening and configured, and nothing has said which process owns the port or where production secrets come from.","to":"With one request now traced from source to socket, the exam asks you to place any failure on that path and say which layer forgot something.","gate":{"q":"Why must an ASP.NET Core application keep Kestrel even when IIS or Nginx sits in front of it?","a":"Because the proxy forwards rather than executes. IIS through the ASP.NET Core Module, and Nginx on Linux, terminate TLS and pass plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, which is the only server that understands the application. That is why forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place."}}],"tw":[{"n":1,"name":"What Is Technical Writing","from":"A pump leaves the shop with a brochure praising its power and a folded sheet naming the inlet, the priming step and the dry-run warning, and only one lets the buyer start it.","to":"That sheet was written for one reader at one moment, which forces the next question: who exactly is the reader, and what single job must the document finish for them?","gate":{"q":"Why is technical writing judged by what the reader can do afterwards rather than by how the prose sounds?","a":"Technical writing exists to change an action, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked."}},{"n":2,"name":"Audience and Purpose","from":"A single sheet cannot serve the first-time installer and the service engineer who arrives three years later, leaving the writer to decide which reader the document is built around.","to":"Knowing the reader and the purpose still leaves a blank page and a deadline, so the question becomes what sequence of stages turns that knowledge into a finished, checkable document.","gate":{"q":"Why must a writer name one primary reader before deciding vocabulary, depth and medium?","a":"The primary reader is the person who must act, so their knowledge and situation set the ceiling on every choice. If the document is built around that reader, vocabulary matches what they know, depth stops where their task ends, and the medium fits where they will read it. A document aimed at everyone drifts toward the average and fails the one reader who must act."}},{"n":3,"name":"The Writing Process","from":"A settled reader and a fixed purpose still leave a blank page and a deadline, and nothing yet says in what order planning, drafting and polishing should happen so that faults are found early.","to":"The five stages produce a document, but most workplace writing never grows beyond a message, and the open question is how a short email, memo or letter earns a busy reader's attention.","gate":{"q":"Why does the writing process place revision before copyediting instead of polishing as you draft?","a":"Each stage needs a different attention, and mixing them does none well. Drafting without judging keeps pace and exposes missing structure, revising then tests content, order and completeness against the purpose, and copyediting fixes grammar and consistency only after the structure has stopped moving. Polishing a section that should be cut is wasted work, so the order protects the deadline."}},{"n":4,"name":"Brief Correspondence","from":"Most workplace writing is a message sent to someone who did not ask for it, and the open question is how the subject line, header and first sentence earn attention in three seconds.","to":"A message that survives a busy inbox is short, but any document longer than a screen must be navigated rather than read, which makes the layout of the page the next problem.","gate":{"q":"Why is a memo structured differently from a business letter?","a":"A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT above the body. A letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed. The distance decides the ceremony."}},{"n":5,"name":"Document Design and Graphics","from":"A message of one screen needs no navigation, but the moment a document runs to several pages the reader is hunting for a figure or a warning instead of reading in order.","to":"A well-designed page still assumes a reader who arrived on purpose, which fails for the visitor who lands from a search result, scans for one answer and leaves without scrolling.","gate":{"q":"Why does a chart need a title, labelled axes with units, a legend and a source?","a":"A reader under pressure goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source let the graphic be understood in isolation, and they also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference. Without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect."}},{"n":6,"name":"Writing for the Web","from":"Print gives a reader a page to hold and a designer a fixed width, while the web gives a visitor one glance, one thumb and no obligation to stay past the first line.","to":"A page answers one visitor at a time, but an organisation must report the same facts every month in a shape that an absent reader can compare across periods.","gate":{"q":"Why must a web page state its answer before its background?","a":"The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen. The inverted pyramid therefore puts the conclusion first, then supporting detail, then background. A page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything, and the visit ends without the answer being read."}},{"n":7,"name":"Information Reports","from":"A web page answers one visitor's question, but a manager who was not at the site needs the same facts recorded in a repeatable shape that can be set beside last month's.","to":"Selecting evidence for an absent reader is tested harder when two hundred files arrive for one post and a stranger must decide in an afternoon whether you can do the job.","gate":{"q":"Why does an information report lose its definition the moment it says we recommend?","a":"An information report presents findings so that whoever holds authority can decide, while a recommendation report performs that deciding. The moment the writer writes we recommend, the document claims authority it was not given, and the reader can no longer separate evidence from judgement. The consequence is that the report is either rejected or acted on as an advisory document it was never scoped to be."}},{"n":8,"name":"Employment Communication","from":"A report is read because the reader needs the facts, while a résumé is read by someone deciding whether to hire you, so every line must answer one advertisement.","to":"A written case can be reread, but a pitch or a viva puts the same argument in front of a room that cannot rewind and can hold one idea at a time.","gate":{"q":"Why is a résumé described as an argument rather than a record of your career?","a":"A résumé is read against one advertised post, by a reader who has twenty seconds and one question: can this person do this job. So it selects evidence rather than listing duties, orders it by whatever makes the strongest case, and quantifies results. A career record explains what happened to you, while a targeted argument answers the employer's need."}},{"n":9,"name":"Presentations","from":"A shortlist earns an interview, and an interview is a presentation under time pressure, so the case already made on paper must now survive being spoken once, in front of people who cannot rewind.","to":"A presentation convinces a room, but a decision that outlives the room needs a document the reader can test later, which raises the question of what a recommendation report must contain.","gate":{"q":"Why does a presentation fail when the speaker tries to say everything the report contains?","a":"An audience holds about one idea at a time, so a talk that carries eleven half-ideas leaves the room with none. The mechanism is that points compete for the same attention, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard. Fixing one core message with three to five evidenced points, signposted and rehearsed to time, is what survives."}},{"n":10,"name":"Recommendation Reports","from":"A talk persuades the people in the room, but the person who signs the budget was absent, and the decision must survive being questioned weeks after everyone has gone home.","to":"Recommending an option assumes the work will happen, but a decision to act is still not a plan, and someone must now promise specific work, a schedule and a price that can be signed.","gate":{"q":"Why must the criteria be fixed before the options are examined?","a":"Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy. Fixing cost, time, risk and support first lets every option be scored against the same standard, and lets the decision-maker argue about the standard once rather than re-litigating each site. The consequence is a recommendation the absent reader can test."}},{"n":11,"name":"Proposals","from":"A recommendation report tells the reader which option to choose, but choosing is not the same as commissioning, and the chosen option still has no named work, no schedule and no price.","to":"Every promise of work, every price and every schedule is written by someone under pressure to win, which raises the question of what the writer must not do when the deadline and the truth disagree.","gate":{"q":"How does a proposal differ from a recommendation report on the same problem?","a":"A recommendation report analyses options and tells the reader what to decide, while a proposal promises that the writer will do specific work for a stated price. So the proposal carries deliverables, method, milestones, named staff, an itemised budget with assumptions and acceptance criteria. The difference matters because a recommendation can be accepted without anyone being commissioned, and a proposal cannot."}},{"n":12,"name":"Ethics in the Workplace","from":"A proposal promises work under pressure to win, so the writer chooses the wording, the scale and the cut-off date that decide whether the reader is informed or merely not lied to.","to":"The paper closes where every document is finally judged, since a report, a proposal and a résumé all stand or fall on whether a reader given only that page would reach the writer's conclusion.","gate":{"q":"Why is a factually accurate document still capable of misleading its reader?","a":"Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document. Omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached."}}],"ecom":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","from":"A shop moves onto a network and loses its counter, its ward licence, its signboard and its cash box at once, leaving an order that only half completes.","to":"It hands forward the question every surviving shop must answer, which is who pays whom, for what, and why that stranger comes back a second time.","gate":{"q":"Why does e-commerce need a five-layer framework rather than simply a website and a delivery van?","a":"A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin."}},{"n":2,"name":"E-commerce Business Model","from":"The five layers say what must exist but never who pays, so a shop can be complete and still never learn whether its buyer returns.","to":"It hands forward the moment the model is tested, when a stranger must actually pay across a network without either side meeting or trusting the other.","gate":{"q":"Why does a market creator carry less inventory risk than an e-tailer while earning only a fee?","a":"An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss. A market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital."}},{"n":3,"name":"Electronic Payment System","from":"A model that names its payer still assumes the money can arrive, and a card number crossing six hands is the account itself in anyone's pocket.","to":"It hands forward one payment made safe, which now has to be built into a store that records the order, holds the stock and proves the money arrived.","gate":{"q":"Why does SET need a dual signature rather than one signature over the whole purchase?","a":"The merchant must read the order and the bank must read the card, yet neither may read both. So the cardholder hashes each half, signs the two digests together, and sends that one signature attached to each half. Altering either half breaks verification, and no party can deny the half it sent."}},{"n":4,"name":"Building E-commerce System","from":"A safe payment still needs a place to happen, because a web page forgets the basket between clicks and only a written order can carry it to the money.","to":"It hands forward an order holding card data, addresses and stock, which makes the shop a far larger target than the single payment it was built around.","gate":{"q":"Why is the browser's return page not proof that a payment succeeded?","a":"That page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads. Payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for."}},{"n":5,"name":"Security in E-Commerce","from":"An order now holds card data, addresses and stock, and a shop stores thousands of such orders, which is a much larger target than one protected payment ever was.","to":"It hands forward a shop that is safe but unknown, because a locked door attracts no customer and someone must now buy the attention of strangers.","gate":{"q":"Why does a hash prove integrity but not authenticity?","a":"A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible. But anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation."}},{"n":6,"name":"Digital Marketing","from":"A safe shop with no visitors earns nothing, and the signboard once nailed to the street must now be rented from an auction closing before the page renders.","to":"It hands forward traffic that must be bought again every month, unless the site can be found without paying for the click and can suggest the next item itself.","gate":{"q":"Why must a campaign be judged on acquisition cost against lifetime value rather than click-through rate?","a":"Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything. Acquisition cost divides total spend by customers won, while lifetime value is what those customers return over their whole relationship. A channel whose cost exceeds that value loses money on every sale, however healthy its click rate looks."}},{"n":7,"name":"Optimizing E-commerce Systems","from":"Paid traffic must be bought again every month, so the shop now needs to be found by a crawler and to guess what a returning visitor will take next.","to":"It hands the whole chain back as one examiner's question, in which a single Nepali shop must be explained from the network down to the visitor who returns.","gate":{"q":"Why does collaborative filtering fail a new visitor while content-based filtering still works?","a":"Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either. Content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates."}}]};
